# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Overrides for the machine-wide gitignore.
#
# The global file at %USERPROFILE%\.gitignore is a Unity one, where .csproj and
# .sln really are generated artifacts and are correctly excluded. For a normal
# .NET repository that rule is fatal: without project files nobody who clones
# this can build it. Repository rules are evaluated after global ones, so these
# put the files back.
#
# The global file is left alone on purpose. It is right for what it was written
# for.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
!*.csproj
!*.sln
!*.slnx
!*.props
!*.targets

# Build output
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
[Dd]ebug/
[Rr]elease/
artifacts/
publish/

# Visual Studio / Rider / VS Code
.vs/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates

# Test and coverage output
[Tt]est[Rr]esult*/
coverage*.json
coverage*.xml
coverage*.info
*.coverage
*.trx

# The one coverage file that is INPUT rather than output: the floors the gate holds the
# measurement against. It has to travel with the repository, because CI reads it and CI cannot
# see tools/. Without this line the rule above hides it, which is how it was found - the file was
# written, committed nothing, and would have left the workflow with no floor to read.
!tests/coverage-floor.json

# NuGet
*.nupkg
packages/

# Local tooling and scratch
.claude/
_scratch/
*.local.json

# Working documentation. Deliberately outside version control: the owner keeps and
# versions it separately. Consequences are spelled out in CLAUDE.md itself, section
# "Dokumenty" - the short version is that git will never show you that a document
# changed, so document changes have to be reported by hand.
#
# CLAUDE.md is excluded for the same reason. It still works: it is read from disk,
# not from git.
#
# A public repository will eventually need its own README, written for contributors
# and independent of these files.
docs/
CLAUDE.md

# Developer tools. Kept out for the same reason and by the same decision (2026-08-01):
# the owner backs these up separately. They are not part of the product, they never ship,
# and ADR-19 does not apply to them - a tool here may do things the program may not.
#
# Same consequence as above and it is the one that bites: git will never show that a tool
# changed, and somebody cloning this repository gets none of them. Anything here that
# matters has to be described in docs/ well enough to be rebuilt from the description.
tools/

# The developer changelog. Taken out of version control on 2026-08-02 by the owner, who
# keeps it beside docs/ rather than in the repository. Its history up to that day stays in
# git's objects and that was considered and accepted - the point is that it stops being a
# tracked file from here on, not that it is erased.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE IS BIGGER THAN THE ONES ABOVE AND HAS TO BE SAID. This file used to be
# the ONE piece of project memory that survived a clone: docs/, CLAUDE.md and tools/ were
# already out. Now nothing does. Somebody cloning this repository gets the code, the user
# changelog, SECURITY.md and the licence - no decisions, no measurements, no backlog, and
# no record of what was already tried and rejected. A public repository will need its own
# writing for contributors, and that is no longer a nicety.
CHANGELOG-DEV.md
